  "meta": {
    "title": "No two 1s in a row",
    "blurb": "Binary strings with no two adjacent 1s. Only the last symbol matters, so two live states and a trap are all it takes — and the number of accepted words of length n is the Fibonacci number F(n+2).",
    "inputs": [
      {
        "w": "",
        "expect": "accept",
        "label": "ε"
      },
      {
        "w": "0",
        "expect": "accept"
      },
      {
        "w": "1",
        "expect": "accept"
      },
      {
        "w": "1010",
        "expect": "accept"
      },
      {
        "w": "10010",
        "expect": "accept"
      },
      {
        "w": "11",
        "expect": "reject"
      },
      {
        "w": "0110",
        "expect": "reject"
      },
      {
        "w": "101011",
        "expect": "reject"
      }
    ],
    "library": {
      "author": {
        "login": "thethinkmachine"
      },
      "license": "CC-BY-4.0",
      "tags": [
        "fibonacci",
        "forbidden-pattern",
        "textbook"
      ],
      "difficulty": "intro",
      "readme": "The language of binary strings that never contain **11**. The machine only has to remember whether the last symbol was a 1: a 0 always resets it, and a second 1 falls into the trap.\n\nCount the accepted words by length — 1, 2, 3, 5, 8, 13, … — and the Fibonacci numbers appear. A word of length n either ends in 0 (any good word of length n−1 followed by 0) or in 01 (any good word of length n−2 followed by 01), which is exactly the recurrence F(n) = F(n−1) + F(n−2).\n\nThe same strings are the *Zeckendorf representations*: every positive integer is a sum of non-consecutive Fibonacci numbers in exactly one way."
    }
  }
}
